              "passageTitle": "The Mother of Trees",
              "text": "In the early 1970s, Wangari Maathai noticed that something was going wrong in her home country, Kenya. Rivers that she remembered from her childhood were drying up, forests were being cut down, and women in the countryside had to walk farther and farther every day to find firewood and clean water. Many families could no longer grow enough food.\n\nMaathai was not an ordinary woman. She was the first woman in East and Central Africa to earn a doctorate degree, and she taught at the University of Nairobi. She believed that the answer to many of these problems was simple: plant trees. Trees would protect the soil, keep water in the ground and give people wood and fruit.\n\nIn 1977 she started the Green Belt Movement. The idea was to pay village women a small amount of money for every young tree they planted that survived. At first, many people laughed at her. Some officials thought that women without education could not do such work. But the women proved them wrong. They collected seeds, grew young trees in small nurseries and planted them across the country.\n\nMaathai's work was not always easy. She often criticised the government when it tried to sell public forests and parks to builders, and she was arrested several times. Yet she never gave up. Over the following decades, the women of the Green Belt Movement planted tens of millions of trees.\n\nIn 2004, Maathai became the first African woman to receive the Nobel Peace Prize. Some people were surprised that **it** was given for planting trees. Maathai explained that peace depends on how we share and protect the Earth's resources. She believed that when people plant trees, they also plant hope for the future.",
